  "resultText": "Clinic cancellations with a refund now refund the no-show invoice. `npm test` passes (32 unit tests and 6 e2e tests), and typecheck and lint are clean.\n\n**What changed**\n- **Cancelling a no-show:** Before, `cancel` only worked on `SCHEDULED` appointments, so cancelling an already-charged no-show would have returned a 422. `Appointment.cancel(reason, { refund })` now also accepts a `NO_SHOW` appointment, but only when the reason is `clinic` and `refund` is `true`. Any other cancel of a no-show is still rejected, and the fee stays open.\n- **Event:** `AppointmentCancelled` has a new `refund: boolean` field. It's true only for a clinic cancellation that asked for a refund. The controller reads `refund` from the request body.\n- **Billing:** An `AppointmentCancelled` listener calls the new `InvoicingService.refundNoShow` when `refund` is true. It does nothing if no fee was charged or if the invoice is already refunded.\n- **Invoice:** The invoice status can now be `REFUNDED`. A second `refund()` on the same invoice throws `InvoiceAlreadyRefundedError`. `PrismaInvoiceRepository.save` is now an upsert that updates only the status, because an issued invoice is otherwise immutable.\n- **Tests:** I added domain tests for appointment cancellation and invoice refund, plus service tests for both, and two e2e tests. One e2e test covers the successful refund. The other checks that a plain `clinic` cancel (no `refund`) of a no-show returns 422 and leaves the invoice `OPEN`.\n\nA clinic cancel with `refund: true` on a still-`SCHEDULED` appointment succeeds as an ordinary cancel. It refunds nothing because no fee exists.\n\nI haven't committed anything.",
